Start();
cout "WO-D windows as setup leaves it" windows:;
windows = 2;
show(firstLight);
search FORMs;
register(WindowOutlineT);
define
    woX = 0;
    woY = 0;
    woRead form:=firstLight;
    woHead form:=firstLight px=250 py=265;
    woBig x=300 y=450 width=640 height=700;
    woResize form:=firstLight frame:=woBig;
    woClose form:=firstLight;
    woAt code={
        px = woX;
        py = woY;
        argument :% copyOf(px);
        argument :% copyOf(py);
        pixelAt(argument);
        };
    woScan code={
        readWindow(woRead);
        woX = 10;
        woY = 265;
        while woX < 490;
            woAt(woRead);
            woX = woX + 4;
        woX = 1; woAt(woRead);
        woX = 498; woAt(woRead);
        };
    ;
search WindowOutlineT;
cout "WO-0 nothing selected":;
woScan();
windowClick(woHead);
cout "WO-1 describeHead selected":;
woScan();
cout "WO-2 a second show of the open form":;
show(firstLight);
windowResize(woResize);
cout "WO-3 closed, then shown again":;
windowClose(woClose);
show(firstLight);
cout "WO END":;
stop();

windowOutlineT -- windows stroke A (2026-11-23, SEQ 757): selection as an outline, and the second show. Tester's shape.
WO-D  windows is 1 before this file sets it (the default, Tony 2026-11-23); the fleet then sets 2.
WO-0  firstLight in a window, nothing selected: a scan across the held form's heading (y 265, every 4 points) reads the
      heading's green and its white text; the two ends of the line (x 1 and 498) are green.
WO-1  the heading clicked: the SAME scan, sample for sample -- the fill is still green and the white text still on it --
      and the two ends are the selection colour: the outline. When selection was a fill the scan read no green at all.
WO-2  show(firstLight) again while its window is open: said by name, no second window; then one resize, of the one window.
WO-3  the window closed and the form shown again: a window is made anew, at the frame the close kept (640x700).
